Flexbox is primarily a one-dimensional layout tool: it distributes and aligns items along a row or column. It is a good fit for navigation links, button groups, and content inside a component. Grid models rows and columns together, so it is usually clearer for page regions or card layouts where both axes matter. They are not competing technologies; a page can use Grid for its major regions and Flexbox inside each region. I choose the simplest model that expresses the relationship, then test long content and narrow widths rather than relying on a screenshot.

```css caption="Grid handles page tracks; Flexbox handles one-axis control alignment."
.page { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; }
.actions { display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; align-items: center; }
```

The cascade first considers origin and importance, then the relevant cascade layer. Specificity is compared among competing declarations in the same applicable origin and layer: IDs outweigh classes, attributes, and pseudo-classes, which outweigh type selectors and pseudo-elements. `:where()` contributes zero specificity, while `:is()`, `:not()`, and `:has()` take specificity from their most specific argument. If specificity ties, scoping proximity and then source order decide. I keep selectors low-weight and use layers or component boundaries instead of escalating specificity or reaching for `!important`.

```css caption="The second selector has greater specificity because :where() contributes zero."
:where(.card) a { color: navy; }
.card a { color: teal; }
```

I use custom properties for shared design values, cascade layers to define broad precedence deliberately, and low-specificity selectors so component styles remain overridable. `:where()` is useful when authoring defaults that should not create a specificity fight; `:has()` can express a relationship when the supported browser target permits it. CSS nesting can reduce repetition, but I still inspect the generated selector specificity. The guardrails are progressive enhancement where necessary, a documented browser-support policy, and tests for focus, zoom, contrast, overflow, and reduced-motion preferences. A newer feature is not automatically a better abstraction.

```css caption="Tokens, layers, and zero-specificity defaults make overrides predictable."
:root { --space-2: 0.5rem; }
@layer reset, components, utilities;
@layer components { :where(.card) { padding: var(--space-2); } }
```
